    "scenario": "BGP Always compare MED",
    "goals": "• Configure EBGP between AS {{as100}} and AS {{as200}}.\n  AS {{as100}}: {{router3_name}}\n  AS {{as200}}: {{router1_name}}\n• Configure EBGP between AS {{as100}} and AS {{as300}}, make sure you have a neighbor relationship between router {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} as well as between router {{router3_name}} and {{router2_name}}.\n  AS {{as300}}: {{router2_name}}, {{router4_name}}\n• Configure IBGP within AS {{as300}}.\n• Make sure all neighbor relationships are working, advertise the correct networks in BGP to achieve full connectivity.\n• Router {{router1_name}} AND {{router4_name}} have the same IP address on the loopback1 interface, advertise this in BGP on both routers.\n• Ensure you can ping the {{router4_loopback1_network}} address from router {{router3_name}}.\n• Take a look at the BGP table with ‘show ip bgp’ on router {{router3_name}}.\n• Which path is being used for the {{router4_loopback1_network}} network?\n• Configure router {{router2_name}} and {{router4_name}} so they send a MED of {{med_low}} for the {{router4_loopback1_network}} network towards router {{router3_name}}.\n• Configure router {{router1_name}} so it sends a MED of {{med_high}} for the {{router4_loopback1_network}} network towards router {{router3_name}}.\n• Check router {{router3_name}} with ‘show ip bgp’ to see the changes you just made, if not do a ‘clear ip bgp *’\n• Even though the MED from AS {{as300}} is better, why is traffic towards {{router4_loopback1_network}} still being sent towards AS {{as200}}?\n• Configure router {{router3_name}} so traffic towards {{router4_loopback1_network}} is sent towards AS {{as300}} by comparing MED across different neighboring ASes, the only attribute you are allowed to change is MED.",
